  - `func (c rowColumns) plain(width int) string`
  - `func (c rowColumns) styled(th theme.Theme, issue *beads.Issue, width int) string`

**The decision this task turns on.** `item.go:118` records why the row is
styled once as a unit: a styled span mid-row emits its own reset, which drops
the outer style for everything after it. That constraint does not go away — it
is worked around by rendering the two cases differently:

- **A selected row stays one styled unit over plain text.** `theme.Selected`
  sets a *background*, and any per-column foreground inside it resets that
  background for the rest of the line, leaving a visible gap. The highlight is
  the signal on that row; it does not also need per-column colour.
- **Every other row is composed column by column.** No background is involved,
  so each segment's own reset is harmless.

That is why composition (`compose` → `rowColumns`) is split from rendering
(`plain` / `styled`): both paths lay out identical text at identical widths,
and only the styling differs. Do not collapse them back into one function.

**Column order and budget** (each field carries its own trailing separator):

```
glyph+" "  id+" "  priority+" "  status(11)+" "  title  "  "+labels   " "+age(4)
```

The age is dropped first when the row is tight, then labels, then the title
truncates. `time.Now()` is read inside `fitAge` rather than captured on the
delegate: the row is re-rendered every frame anyway, so ages stay current
without a reload, and `RelativeAge` itself is pure and exhaustively tested by
Task 3.2 — the untested surface is one call.

