**Three rendering paths, not two.** The list has two — selected rows under one
style, everything else per column. The tree has three, because it also mutes a
row retained only so a matching descendant stays reachable. That mutedness is
a statement about the whole row, not about any one column, so it takes the
single-style path just as selection does:

- selected → `th.Selected.Render(prefix + cols.Plain(remaining))`
- retained non-matching ancestor → `th.Muted.Render(prefix + cols.Plain(remaining))`
- otherwise → `th.Base.Render(prefix) + cols.Styled(th, issue, remaining)`

The prefix is rendered by the caller, not carried in `Columns`, which is why
`Plain`/`Styled` are handed `remaining` rather than the full width. `prefix +
Plain(remaining)` is exactly `width` cells.

**The ladder.** The tree's prefix grows with depth, so the title — the only
column that identifies a row — is what must survive. Sacrifice in order:
**age, then status, then id, then the title truncates.** `fitTitle` and
`minTitleWidth` keep their current meaning: they decide which layout is
preferred, not whether a title renders at all.

